{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016bAk1id3iCjBLqZfgE8BoK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Structured Prompt: Shear Strain History from Seismic Base Acceleration\n2\t\n3\t## Context\n4\t\n5\tThe overarching research objective is to formulate a simple proxy model for predicting pore pressure increase in a saturated fine-grained material subjected to a seismic acceleration record. The physical configuration consists of a homogeneous, fine-grained, saturated stratum of thickness $H_s$ resting on rock. The stratum exhibits depth-variable shear stiffness $G_o(z)$, with its maximum value $G_o = G(z=0)$ at the base. A seismic acceleration history $a_g(t)$ is applied at the base of the stratum. The KB/ directory contains analytical closed-form solutions for the site response, expressed in terms of instantaneous accelerations $a(z,t)$ at arbitrary depths within the stratum.\n6\t\n7\t\n8\t## SLOTS\n9\t\n10\t### SLOT 1: Numerical R algorithm for instantaneous shear strain history\n11\t\n12\tThe required deliverable is a numerical algorithm implemented in R that, given a base acceleration time history $a_g(t)$, estimates the instantaneous shear strain history $\\gamma(z,t)$ at any depth $z$ within the stratum. The computational procedure involves two coupled steps: (a) computing the instantaneous acceleration field $a(z,t)$ at any depth $z$ from the base excitation $a_g(t)$, using the analytical closed-form site response solutions available in KB/; and (b) deriving the instantaneous shear strain history $\\gamma(z,t)$ from the computed acceleration field $a(z,t)$. The R implementation must use `data.table()` for data handling and must avoid snake_case naming convention throughout the code.\n13\t\n14\t\n15\t## CONSTRAINTS\n16\t\n17\t- Output language: English.\n18\t- Document style: professional engineering methodology.\n19\t\n20\t\n21\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b88f8d70-df56-48bb-9c8d-c1d386058401","uuid":"f379f69e-1cd1-4c26-afbb-7c29495b310f","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:21:33.503Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/poreB/md/task.poreB.prompt.md","content":"# Structured Prompt: Shear Strain History from Seismic Base Acceleration\n\n## Context\n\nThe overarching research objective is to formulate a simple proxy model for predicting pore pressure increase in a saturated fine-grained material subjected to a seismic acceleration record. The physical configuration consists of a homogeneous, fine-grained, saturated stratum of thickness $H_s$ resting on rock. The stratum exhibits depth-variable shear stiffness $G_o(z)$, with its maximum value $G_o = G(z=0)$ at the base. A seismic acceleration history $a_g(t)$ is applied at the base of the stratum. The KB/ directory contains analytical closed-form solutions for the site response, expressed in terms of instantaneous accelerations $a(z,t)$ at arbitrary depths within the stratum.\n\n\n## SLOTS\n\n### SLOT 1: Numerical R algorithm for instantaneous shear strain history\n\nThe required deliverable is a numerical algorithm implemented in R that, given a base acceleration time history $a_g(t)$, estimates the instantaneous shear strain history $\\gamma(z,t)$ at any depth $z$ within the stratum. The computational procedure involves two coupled steps: (a) computing the instantaneous acceleration field $a(z,t)$ at any depth $z$ from the base excitation $a_g(t)$, using the analytical closed-form site response solutions available in KB/; and (b) deriving the instantaneous shear strain history $\\gamma(z,t)$ from the computed acceleration field $a(z,t)$. The R implementation must use `data.table()` for data handling and must avoid snake_case naming convention throughout the code.\n\n\n## CONSTRAINTS\n\n- Output language: English.\n- Document style: professional engineering methodology.\n\n\n","numLines":21,"startLine":1,"totalLines":21}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Jq4GLSnV3DLRRcY67X4SwF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The manifest says the KB document is `models.md` and output is `poreB_noorch.research.md`. The task file has a structured prompt with SLOTS and CONSTRAINTS.\n\nSLOT 1: Numerical R algorithm for instantaneous shear strain history\n- Given: base acceleration time history $a_g(t)$\n- Compute: instantaneous shear strain history $\\gamma(z,t)$ at depth $z$\n- Steps: (a) compute $a(z,t)$ from $a_g(t)$ using KB analytical solutions, (b) derive $\\gamma(z,t)$ from $a(z,t)$\n\n\n- I'll use `data.table()` for the implementation and stick with camelCase naming conventions. Now let me pull up the KB document to understand the analytical solutions I need to work with.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1085,"cache_read_input_tokens":39245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1085,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b88f8d70-df56-48bb-9c8d-c1d386058401","uuid":"6b54f141-7f1c-4e53-acf8-5bd36d339cc2"}
